930 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 478 0 R
/E (How to improve the analytical model: Frequency offset problem)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
931 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2242 0 R
/E (Pre-Transition Thermal Softening: The theoretical piecewise model assumes a constant Martensite stiffness \(E = Em\) for all temperatures below As. It fundamentally ignores the macroscopic thermal softening that lowers the physical natural frequency prior to phase transformation.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
932 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2243 0 R
/E (Idealized Clamped Boundary: The Euler-Bernoulli mathematical matrix relies on a perfectly rigid wall. The physical laboratory setup contains microscopic mechanical compliance at the clamp.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
933 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2244 0 R
/E (Unknown parameters: The length of the beam used in the test was not measured so the length mentioned on the paper was used instead, also the dimensions for the tip mass were not measured, hence the moment of inertia in the boundary conditions are approximations.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
934 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 479 0 R
/E (POLITECNICO DI MILANO)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
935 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 479 0 R
/E (How to improve the analytical model: Shape of the peak problem)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
936 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2245 0 R
/E (Multi-Axis Coupling & Asymmetry \(Double Peaks\): The theoretical model assumes a perfectly straight, symmetrical beam vibrating exclusively in a single transversal plane. In reality, the tip mass causes an initial static bending. This static curvature, combined with microscopic clamping asymmetries, causes orthogonal bending and torsional modes to couple with the primary bending axis, creating the overlapping "double peaks" observed in the experimental FRFs.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
937 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2246 0 R
/E (Linear Damping Interpolation: The theoretical model applies a simplified linear interpolation between       and     . This mathematical approach misses the complex, mixed-phase boundary friction that causes severe damping spikes during the physical transition \(particularly in Mode 2\).)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [3]
>>
