1003 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2260 0 R
/E (The non-linearity and S-curve phenomenon: The relationship between the electrical current input \(I\) and the resulting natural frequency \(f\) is highly non-linear, taking the shape of a steep S-curve. )
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1004 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2261 0 R
/E (The transition zone sensitivity: The system gain is very high in the phase transformation curve, so a small variation in current results in a strong jump in frequency)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1005 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2262 0 R
/E (Feedback Vulnerability: Feedback-only loops suffer from severe thermal lag, leading to saturation and limit-cycle chatter during large setpoint steps.)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1006 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2263 0 R
/E (Solution)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1007 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 488 0 R
/E (One of the possible solutions is feedforward control. Unlike feedback control, feedforward is predictive. It measures the dominant frequency in the system and immediately calculates the required current.)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1008 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2264 0 R
/E (Implementation)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1009 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 488 0 R
/E (Mapping out the steady-state frequency achieved at each current step, and inverting it mathematically gives a polynomial function )
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
